[Music]  I've always liked Peter Pan he's my  favorite keeps me young keeps me having  fun keeps me focused on not all work all  the time you have to relax and you have  to enjoy live as well hi I'm Kristy and  I live in Georgetown Kentucky I'm a  medical coder with Georgetown Hospital  so I bought a home here in Georgetown  back in 2003 bought my house in July I  made one payment and I got called in to  the office at work and was told that  they were doing away with my position  and that was it so I had 29 years and 11  months of mortgage payments and no job a  single so it was 100% income loss to the  household it's a very scary time I knew  I had to do something  to get moving I couldn't just sit around  and be bitter and disappointed so I  found a job here locally I had a bank  and I was making $8 an hour it was  enough to pay the bills never missed a  payment never got behind on anything it  was good I was in a Bible study and they  were gonna be starting FPU after going  through after you you know I refused to  go into debt so if I didn't have the  money for something I just didn't do it  realized that an emergency fund probably  be a real good idea it having been  through what I had been through  I worked after-hours at Cracker Barrel  in the store just a couple nights a week  and was able to build up my six-month  emergency fund with just that money that  I thought you know I I could do better  for myself so I started looking around  for another job and I found one in town  that was almost a fifty percent pay  increase in a completely different field  I went into the medical field with  Georgetown Hospital so I had never been  at the point where I had this much money  ever the first year I sort of played  around with it put a little extra on the  house every month or so that was in 2014  and then in 2015  I started making double mortgage  payments so when I got more I kept  living on less and just applied the  extra to the mortgage payment so 2016  came and I started making triple  mortgage payments I hadn't even missed  anything it was just how I lived and my  goal I was so focused on my goal that  was the only that was the only thing I  saw you know I just had blinders on  looking at that gotta pay off the house  gotta pay off the house got to make the  mortgage payments everything became a  choice of what I rather do this or would  I rather pay off the house certainly  would I rather go to a movie or what I  rather pay off the house early would I  rather go out to eat or would I rather  pay out that everything everything came  down to that choice so I was gizelle  intense on my house because that's the  only debt I had and I I wanted to be rid  of it so I lived on about $3,600 for the  year in 2016  I had my mortgage calculator up I just  kept it up on my computer at work so  every day I could look at it just to  keep me motivated to keep me going I'm  almost there I'm almost there by the  time I got down to 2017 it was it was  small enough balance that I was getting  excited and I was able to post the last  payment to my account on September 14th  of 2017 and I actually I'm old school I  mailed it I mailed my payment and I went  to the post office that it was a  Wednesday morning and I looked up  outside the post office and the flag was  was blowing and I thought this is  freedom this is what true freedom feels  like and and I just had this weight that  was just lifted off of me and after  everything I've been through and how  hard I had fought in 33 months of these  extra payments and I was done I was  finally done and the next day I checked  my account online and the payment had  posted and I just stared at the balance  being zero it really was just surreal I  was excited but it was it's like I  couldn't quite grasp I don't I own my  house I don't have a house payment  there's not many people in America can  say I don't have a house payment it was  totally worth it at that point and I'm  done forever  [Music]  if I can do it anybody can do it  you know people will tell me yeah but  you don't have kids you don't know what  it's like I don't have a double income  household either I don't make forty  grand a year either  I started at $8 an hour and was able to  live and you know have a home got up to  ten dollars ten fifty an hour was able  to buy a car with cash and then you know  at 26,000 take home I paid off my house  I paid off more in that thirty three  months of being focused than I did the  previous twelve years combined it's just  a matter of staying focused setting your  goals and being willing to do whatever  it takes to reach those goals when I pay  off my house I was gonna go to Disney  World I'm going to Disney World this  year was it sunny if I can get mom to  agree I'm taking my parents actually  when I started paying off the house I  thought you know maybe when I pay off my  house I can take them to Disney World  that would be fun  and I can't wait to take a debt-free  trip to Disney World for a week stand  the resort's there and just in the days  having fun with no debt completely free  enjoying life it's gonna be awesome  [Music]

